            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"A transformed identity, new character, and change in status signifying divine ownership and a new destiny ordained by God.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Revelation 2:17\",\"Revelation 3:12\",\"Isaiah 62:2\",\"Genesis 17:5\",\"Genesis 32:28\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A legal declaration of justification, full pardon, or adoption into God's family, reflecting ancient customs of giving a new name upon adoption or acquittal.\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 2:17\",\"Isaiah 56:5\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Public eschatological vindication and the reversal of past shame, representing a restoration of status before the nations.\",\"basis\":[\"Isaiah 62:2\",\"Isaiah 65:15\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A private revelation of Christ's own nature and unsearchable character given intimately to the victorious believer.\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 3:12\",\"Revelation 19:12\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While the exact cultural referent of the 'new name' on the white stone in Revelation 2:17 is debated among commentators (e.g., an admission ticket to the messianic banquet, a token of legal acquittal, or a victorious athlete's reward), the overarching biblical theology of receiving a 'new name' consistently points to God graciously transforming a person's core identity and claiming them as His own.\"}",
